    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "## Data excluded\n",
        "* 82 orders had no country (6.17%) and 2 had no units sold, so they were dropped. Their value was revenue \\$103.1M, cost \\$75.4M, profit \\$27.7M, about 7% of analysed revenue. The real totals are therefore roughly 7% higher than reported, and the regional shares below leave these orders out.\n",
        "* Year 2017 is also excluded as it isn't full. Meaning that it wil affect data until it stops which skews trends.\n",
        "\n",
        "## Products\n",
        "* Order counts are almost equal across categories (82–104 each), but profit is not. Popularity does not drive profitability.\n",
        "* Best: cosmetics earn the most profit (\\~\\$77M, \\~39% margin) from below-average order counts. Clothes have the highest margin (\\~\\$41M profit on \\~\\$60M revenue, \\~67%).\n",
        "* Worst: office supplies have the highest revenue (\\~\\$345M) but only a ~19% margin. Meat has \\~\\$183M revenue but only ~\\$24M profit (\\~13%). Fruits are the smallest category (\\~\\$4M revenue, \\~\\$1M profit).\n",
        "* Recommendation: review pricing and costs for meat and office supplies to raise margin, and look for ways to grow fruits and beverages (\\~\\$23M revenue, \\~\\$8M profit).\n",
        "\n",
        "## Regions\n",
        "* Europe provides Most of the revenue and profit. Southern Europe leads (\\~\\$512M revenue, ~\\$153M profit), followed by Eastern (\\~\\$354M), Northern (\\~\\$275M) and Western Europe (\\~\\$244M).\n",
        "* The top 10 countries each bring \\$41–51M revenue and \\$11–15M profit, led by the Czech Republic, Bosnia and Herzegovina and Portugal. No single country dominates.\n",
        "* Asia is only (\\~\\$85M revenue, \\~\\$24M profit) and comes from 3 countries: Armenia, Georgia and Cyprus. Its margin (\\~28%) is close to Europe's (\\~30%), so expanding there is a low-risk way to grow beyond a saturated Europe.\n",
        "\n",
        "**Sales channels**\n",
        "* Online and offline are almost identical. Online brings ~\\$728M revenue and ~\\$217M profit, and offline ~\\$743M and ~\\$220M, both at a ~30% margin. The cost of running each channel should be compared.\n",
        "* Product mix differs by channel. Offline sells ~95K more units of baby food and ~55K more of office supplies, while online leads in beverages, cereal, household and snacks. Understanding why could let stock be allocated better and lower costs.\n",
        "\n",
        "**Delivery and timing**\n",
        "* Delivery time has no effect on profit (correlation 0.05), so it should not be prioritised over the points above. The exception is Hungary, the slowest country, where a new delivery provider could be considered.\n",
        "* Saturday and Sunday are the busiest days (~0.47–0.48 orders per category per day versus ~0.41 on Thursday and Friday). The gap is modest, but a weekend promotion is reasonable, and Friday is already strong for cosmetics (0.07)."
      ],
